{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And the Tabernacle was taken down, and the sons of Gershon and Merari set out</b> – The meaning of the verses in this matter is that after the standard of the camp of Judah set out, they would begin dismantling the Tabernacle. The beginning of its dismantling was that Aaron and his sons would come and take down the screening curtain, and this is what is said: \"And Aaron and his sons shall come, when the camp sets out, and they shall take down\" etc. (Numbers 4:5), meaning when the first camp sets out. And when they finish covering the Ark and all the sacred implements — the burden of the sons of Kohath — the Levites would take down the entire Tabernacle and load it onto the wagons, and the sons of Gershon and the sons of Merari would set out after the standard of the camp of Judah, while the Ark and the sacred implements stood covered and placed on their carrying poles until the standard of Reuben set out. And the meaning of \"Then the tent of meeting shall set out with the camp of the Levites in the midst of the camps\" (Numbers 2:17) would be that the tent of meeting and the entire camp of the Levites would set out in the midst of the aforementioned camps: the Gershonites and the Merarites travel between the standard of Judah and the standard of Reuben, and the Kohathites travel between the standard of Reuben and the standard of Ephraim, as he will explain further. And the verse mentions the reason, that they would set up the Tabernacle before their arrival (Numbers 10:21).\n\nBut in the Baraita of the Making of the Tabernacle (chapter 13) I found this language: They sounded a tekiah, a teruah, and a tekiah, and the standard of Judah set out first, as it is said: \"And the standard of the camp of the children of Judah set out first\" (Numbers 10:14). The sons of Aaron entered and took down the curtain and covered the Ark with it, as it is said: \"And Aaron and his sons shall come, when the camp sets out\" etc. (Numbers 4:5). They sounded a tekiah, a teruah, and a tekiah, and the standard of the camp of Reuben set out. Immediately the sons of Gershon and the sons of Merari entered and dismantled the Tabernacle and loaded it onto the wagons and kept it standing until the arrival of the sons of Kohath, as it is said: \"And the Kohathites, the bearers of the sanctuary, set out, and they erected the Tabernacle before their arrival\" (Numbers 10:21). They sounded a tekiah, a teruah, and a tekiah, and the standard of Ephraim set out. Immediately the sons of Kohath entered and dismantled the sanctuary and bore it on the shoulder, as it is said: \"And when Aaron and his sons have finished\" etc. (Numbers 4:15).\n\nAnd perhaps this is its explanation: They sounded a tekiah, a teruah, and a tekiah for the setting out of the standard of Reuben, and immediately the sons of Gershon and the sons of Merari would go first and dismantle the Tabernacle and set out before the sons of Reuben. And likewise they sounded a tekiah, a teruah, and a tekiah for the standard of Ephraim to set out, and the sons of Kohath would go first and bear the sacred implements on their shoulders and set out before the sons of Ephraim. This teaches about the setting out of the Levites that it was with the sounding of blasts, and immediately thereafter the setting out of the standard that came after them."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "In the last paragraph, Ramban proposes a reinterpretation of the Baraita's sequence: rather than the Levites dismantling after each standard departed, they would begin dismantling immediately upon the blasts, before the next standard actually set out, so as to travel ahead of it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:12.921425442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}